---
description: Plain-English comments; wrap at phrases (soft ~80, hard 120)
alwaysApply: true
---

# Comments

Write every comment so tomorrow-you does not WTF: maximum possible plain English,
no jargon, abbreviations, or clever phrasing to decode.

Use the host language's comment syntax (`//` / `/** */` in JS/TS, `<!-- -->` in HTML,
`/* */` in CSS). One line stays one line. More than one line uses that language's
block form — in JS/TS, `/** */` with one `*`-prefixed line per line of text.

```ts
// one line is fine

/**
 * More than one line goes in a block,
 * not stacked `//` lines.
 */
```

## Wrap

Soft cap ~80 characters, hard cap 120. Prefer a shorter line when the point is
already clear. Run a bit past 80 when that keeps a phrase intact.

Break between phrases: after a comma or period; before a coordinating *and* /
*but* / *or* / *so* that starts a new clause. Keep a number with its unit
(`100ms`), a slash-pair (`light/dark`), and a possessive with its noun
(`browser's name`).
